\mt1 Philippians
\c 1
\s1 Greetings from Paul and Timothy
\r (\ref Colossians 1:1–2|COL 1:1-2\ref*; \ref Philemon 1:1–3|PHM 1-3\ref*)
\p \v 1 Paul and Timothy, servants of Christ Jesus,
\p To all the saints in Christ Jesus at Philippi, together with \add the\add* overseers and deacons:
\p \v 2 Grace and peace to you from God our Father and \add the\add* Lord Jesus Christ.
\s1 Thanksgiving and Prayer
\r (\ref 1 Corinthians 1:4–9|1CO 1:4-9\ref*; \ref Colossians 1:3–14|COL 1:3-14\ref*)
\p \v 3 I thank my God every time I remember you. \v 4 In every prayer for all of you, I always pray with joy, \v 5 because of your partnership in the gospel from the first day until now, \v 6 being confident of this, that He who began a good work in you will carry it on to completion until \add the\add* day of Christ Jesus.
\p \v 7 It is right for me to feel this way about all of you, since I have you in \add my\add* heart. \add For\add* in my chains and in \add my\add* defense and confirmation of the gospel, you are all partners in grace with me. \v 8 God \add is\add* my witness how I long for all of you with \add the\add* affection of Christ Jesus.
\p \v 9 And this \add is\add* my prayer: that your love may abound more and more in knowledge and depth of insight, \v 10 so that you may be able to test and prove what is best \add and\add* may be pure and blameless for \add the\add* day of Christ, \v 11 filled with \add the\add* fruit of righteousness that \add comes\add* through Jesus Christ, to \add the\add* glory and praise of God.
\s1 Paul’s Trials Advance the Gospel
\r (\ref James 1:2–12|JAS 1:2-12\ref*)
\p \v 12 Now I want you to know, brothers, that my circumstances \add have\add* actually served to advance the gospel. \v 13 As a result, it has become clear throughout the whole palace guard\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Or \fqa all the Praetorium\f* and to everyone else \add that\add* I am in chains for Christ. \v 14 And most of the brothers, confident in \add the\add* Lord by my chains, \add now\add* dare more greatly to speak the word\f + \fr 1:14 \ft NE and WH \fqa the word of God\f* without fear.
\p \v 15 It is true that some preach Christ out of envy and rivalry, but \add others\add* out of goodwill. \v 16 The \add latter\add* do so in love, knowing that I am appointed for \add the\add* defense of the gospel.\f + \fr 1:16 \ft BYZ and TR reverse the order of verses 16 and 17.\f* \v 17 The \add former\add*, however, preach Christ out of selfish ambition, not sincerely, supposing that they can add to \add the\add* distress of my chains.
\p \v 18 What then  \add is the issue \add*?\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Literally \fqa What then?\f* Just this: that in every way, whether by false motives or true, Christ is preached. And in this I rejoice. Yes, and I will continue to rejoice, \v 19 because I know that through your prayers and \add the\add* provision of the Spirit of Jesus Christ, \add my distress \add* will turn out for my deliverance. \v 20 I eagerly expect and hope that I will in no way be ashamed, but will have complete boldness so that now as always Christ will be exalted in my body, whether by life or by death.
\s1 To Live Is Christ
\p \v 21 For to me, to live \add is\add* Christ, and to die \add is\add* gain. \v 22 But if I go on living in \add the\add* body, this \add will mean\add* fruitful labor for me. So what shall I choose? I do not know. \v 23 I am torn between the two. I desire to depart and be with Christ, \add which is\add* far better indeed. \v 24 But \add it is\add* more necessary for you that I remain in the body.
\p \v 25 Convinced of this, I know that I will remain and will continue with all of you for your progress and joy in the faith, \v 26 so that through my coming to you again your exultation in Christ Jesus will resound on account of me.
\s1 Worthy of the Gospel
\p \v 27 Nevertheless, conduct yourselves in a manner worthy of the gospel of Christ. Then, whether I come and see you or only hear about you in my absence, \add I will know\add* that you stand firm in one spirit, contending together as one\f + \fr 1:27 \ft Or \fqa striving together with one mind\f* for the faith of the gospel, \v 28 without being frightened in \add any way\add* by those who oppose \add you\add*. \add This\add* is a clear sign of their destruction but of your salvation, and \add it is\add* from God. \v 29 For it has been granted to you on behalf of Christ not only to believe in Him, but also to suffer for Him, \v 30 \add since\add* you are encountering the same struggle you saw I had, and \add now\add* hear that I still have.
\c 2
\s1 One in Christ
\r (\ref Ephesians 2:11–18|EPH 2:11-18\ref*)
\p \v 1 Therefore if \add you have\add* any encouragement in Christ, if any comfort from \add His\add* love, if any fellowship with \add the\add* Spirit, if any affection and compassion, \v 2 \add then make\add* my joy complete by being like-minded, having the same love, being united in spirit \add and\add* purpose.
\p \v 3 \add Do\add* nothing out of selfish ambition or empty pride, but in humility consider others more important than yourselves. \v 4 Each \add of you\add* should look not only \add to\add* your own interests, but also to the interests of others.
\s1 The Mind of Christ
\r (\ref Isaiah 52:13–15|ISA 52:13-15\ref*)
\p \v 5 Let this mind be in you which \add was\add* also in Christ Jesus:
\b 
\q1 \v 6 Who, existing in \add the\add* form of God,
\q2 \add did\add* not consider equality with God
\q2 something to be grasped,\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Or \fqa something to be exploited\f*
\q1 \v 7 but emptied Himself,
\q2 taking \add the\add* form of a servant,
\q2 being made in human likeness.
\q1 \v 8 And being found in appearance as a man,
\q2 He humbled Himself
\q1 \add and\add* became obedient to death —
\q2 even death on \add a\add* cross.
\b 
\q1 \v 9 Therefore God exalted Him \add to the highest place\add*
\q2 and gave Him the name above all names,
\q1 \v 10 that at the name of Jesus every knee should bow,
\q2 in heaven and on earth and under the earth,
\q1 \v 11 and every tongue confess that Jesus Christ \add is\add* Lord,
\q2 to \add the\add* glory of God \add the\add* Father.
\s1 Lights in the World
\r (\ref Matthew 5:13–16|MAT 5:13-16\ref*)
\p \v 12 Therefore, my beloved, just as you have always obeyed, not only in my presence, but now even more in my absence, continue to work out your salvation with fear and trembling. \v 13 For it is God who works in you to will and to act on behalf of \add His\add* good purpose.
\p \v 14 Do everything without complaining \add or\add* arguing, \v 15 so that you may be blameless and pure, children of God without fault in a crooked and perverse generation,\f + \fr 2:15 \ref Deuteronomy 32:5|DEU 32:5\ref*\f* in which you shine as lights in \add the\add* world \v 16 as you hold forth \add the\add* word of life, in order that I may boast on \add the\add* day of Christ that I did not run \add or\add* labor in vain.
\p \v 17 But even if I am being poured out like a drink offering on the sacrifice and service of your faith, I am glad and rejoice with all of you. \v 18 So you too should be glad and rejoice with me.
\s1 Timothy and Epaphroditus
\r (\ref 1 Corinthians 16:10–12|1CO 16:10-12\ref*)
\p \v 19 Now I hope in \add the\add* Lord Jesus to send Timothy to you soon, that I also may be cheered when I learn how you \add are doing\add*. \v 20 I have nobody else like him who \add will\add* genuinely care for your needs. \v 21 For all the \add others\add* look after their own interests, not those of Jesus Christ. \v 22 But you know \add Timothy’s\add* proven worth, that as a child \add with his\add* father he has served with me to \add advance\add* the gospel. \v 23 So I hope to send him as soon as I see what happens with me. \v 24 And I trust in \add the\add* Lord that I myself will come soon.
\p \v 25 But I thought \add it\add* necessary to send back to you Epaphroditus, my brother, fellow worker, and fellow soldier, \add who is\add* also your messenger and minister to my needs. \v 26 For he has been longing for all of you and is distressed because you heard he was ill. \v 27 He was sick indeed, nearly unto death. But God had mercy on him, and not only on him but also on me, to spare me sorrow upon sorrow.
\p \v 28 Therefore \add I am\add* all the more eager to send him, so that when you see him again you may rejoice, and I may be less anxious. \v 29 Welcome him in \add the\add* Lord with great joy, and honor men \add like him\add*, \v 30 because he nearly died for the work of Christ, risking \add his\add* life to make up for your deficit of service to me.
\c 3
\s1 Righteousness through Faith in Christ
\r (\ref Romans 3:21–31|ROM 3:21-31\ref*)
\p \v 1 Finally, my brothers, rejoice in \add the\add* Lord. \add It is\add* no trouble for me to write the same things to you again, and \add it is\add* a safeguard for you.
\p \v 2 Watch out for those dogs, those workers of evil, those mutilators of the flesh! \v 3 For \add it is\add* we \add who\add* are the circumcision, we who worship by \add the\add* Spirit of God, who glory in Christ Jesus, and \add who\add* put no confidence in \add the\add* flesh — \v 4 though I myself could have such confidence.
\p If anyone else thinks he has grounds for confidence in \add the\add* flesh, I \add have\add* more: \v 5 circumcised on \add the\add* eighth day, of \add the\add* people of Israel, of \add the\add* tribe of Benjamin; a Hebrew of Hebrews; as to \add the\add* law, a Pharisee; \v 6 as to zeal, persecuting the church; as to righteousness in \add the\add* law, faultless.
\p \v 7 But whatever was gain to me I count as loss for the sake of Christ. \v 8 More than that, I count all things as loss compared to the surpassing excellence of knowing Christ Jesus my Lord, for whom I have lost all things. I consider \add them\add* rubbish, that I may gain Christ \v 9 and be found in Him, not having my own righteousness from \add the\add* law, but that which \add is\add* through faith in Christ,\f + \fr 3:9 \ft Or \fqa through the faithfulness of Christ\f* the righteousness from God on the basis of faith.
\p \v 10 \add I want\add* to know \add Christ\add* and the power of His resurrection and the fellowship of His sufferings, being conformed to \add Him\add* in His death, \v 11 and so, somehow, to attain to the resurrection from \add the\add* dead.
\s1 Pressing on toward the Goal
\p \v 12 Not that I have already obtained \add all this\add*, or have already been made perfect, but I press on to take hold of that for which Christ Jesus took hold of me. \v 13 Brothers, I \add do\add* not consider myself yet to have taken hold \add of it\add*. But one thing \add I do\add*: Forgetting what is behind and straining toward what \add is\add* ahead, \v 14 I press on toward \add the\add* goal to \add win\add* the prize of God’s heavenly calling in Christ Jesus.
\p \v 15 All of us who \add are\add* mature should embrace this point of view. And if you think differently about some issue, God will reveal this to you as well. \v 16 Nevertheless, we must live up to what we have already attained.
\s1 Citizenship in Heaven
\p \v 17 Join one another in following my \add example\add*, brothers, and carefully observe those who walk according to the pattern we set for you. \v 18 For \add as\add* I have often told you \add before\add*, and now say \add again\add* even with tears: Many live \add as\add* enemies of the cross of Christ. \v 19 \add Their\add* end \add is\add* destruction, \add their\add* god \add is their\add* belly, and \add their\add* glory \add is\add* in their shame. Their minds are set on earthly things.
\p \v 20 But our citizenship is in heaven, and we eagerly await a Savior from \add there\add*, \add the\add* Lord Jesus Christ, \v 21 who, by the power that enables Him to subject all things to Himself, will transform our lowly bodies to be like His glorious body.
\c 4
\s1 Rejoice in the Lord
\p \v 1 Therefore, my brothers, whom I love and long for, my joy and crown, that is how you must stand firm in \add the\add* Lord, my beloved.
\p \v 2 I urge Euodia and Syntyche to agree \add with each other\add* in \add the\add* Lord. \v 3 Yes, and I ask you, \add my\add* true yokefellow,\f + \fr 4:3 \ft Or \fqa I ask you, loyal Syzygus\f* to help these \add women\add* who have contended at my side for the gospel, along with Clement and the rest of my fellow workers, whose names \add are\add* in \add the\add* Book of Life.
\p \v 4 Rejoice in \add the\add* Lord always. I will say \add it\add* again: Rejoice! \v 5 \add Let\add* your gentleness be apparent to all. The Lord \add is\add* near.
\p \v 6 Be anxious for nothing, but in everything, by prayer and petition, with thanksgiving, present your requests to God. \v 7 And the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and your minds in Christ Jesus.
\p \v 8 Finally, brothers, whatever is true, whatever \add is\add* honorable, whatever \add is\add* right, whatever \add is\add* pure, whatever \add is\add* lovely, whatever \add is\add* admirable — if anything \add is\add* excellent \add or\add* praiseworthy — think on these things. \v 9 Whatever you have learned or received or heard \add from me\add*, or seen in me, put it into practice. And the God of peace will be with you.
\s1 The Generosity of the Philippians
\r (\ref 2 Corinthians 8:1–15|2CO 8:1-15\ref*)
\p \v 10 Now I rejoice greatly in \add the\add* Lord that at last you have revived \add your\add* concern for me. You were indeed concerned, but you had no opportunity \add to show it\add*. \v 11 I am not saying this out of need, for I have learned to be content regardless of my circumstances. \v 12 I know \add how\add* to live humbly, and I know \add how\add* to abound. In any and every situation I have learned the secret of being filled and being hungry, of having plenty and having need. \v 13 I can do all things through \add Christ\add* who gives me strength.\f + \fr 4:13 \ft NA, SBL, NE, and WH \fqa in the One who gives me strength. \ft BYZ and TR \fqa in Christ who gives me strength.\f*
\p \v 14 Nevertheless, you have done well to share in my affliction. \v 15 And as you Philippians know, in \add the\add* early days of the gospel, when I left Macedonia, no church but you partnered with me in the matter of giving and receiving. \v 16 For even \add while I was\add* in Thessalonica, you provided for my needs again and again.
\p \v 17 Not that I am seeking a gift, but I am looking for the fruit that may be credited to your account. \v 18 I have all I need and more, now that I have received your gifts from Epaphroditus. \add They are\add* a fragrant \add offering\add*, an acceptable sacrifice, well-pleasing to God.
\p \v 19 And my God will supply all your needs according to His glorious riches in Christ Jesus. \v 20 To our God and Father \add be\add* glory forever \add and\add* ever. Amen.
\s1 Final Greetings
\r (\ref Ephesians 6:21–24|EPH 6:21-24\ref*; \ref 2 Timothy 4:19–22|2TI 4:19-22\ref*)
\p \v 21 Greet all the saints in Christ Jesus.
\p The brothers who are with me send you greetings.
\p \v 22 All the saints send you greetings, especially those from the household of Caesar.
\p \v 23 The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ \add be\add* with your spirit.\f + \fr 4:23 \ft BYZ and TR include \fqa Amen.\f*
